probability -- cumulative distribution function
***********************************************

  * Usage: 
        probability_X x
  * Inputs:
      * X, a *note probability distribution: ProbabilityDistribution,, 
      * x, a *note real number: (Macaulay2Doc)RR,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * LowerTail => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true
  * Outputs:
      * a *note real number: (Macaulay2Doc)RR,, 

Description
===========

The cumulative distribution function (see
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/cumulative_distribution_function ) of the
probability distribution, i.e., the lower tail probability \(F_X(x) = P(X \leq
x)\).

+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : Z = normalDistribution()         |
|                                      |
|o1 = N(0,1)                           |
|                                      |
|o1 : ContinuousProbabilityDistribution|
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : probability_Z 1.96               |
|                                      |
|o2 = .9750021048517796                |
|                                      |
|o2 : RR (of precision 53)             |
+--------------------------------------+

If the LowerTail option is false, then it instead computes the value of the
survival function (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/survival_function ), i.e.,
the upper tail probability \(S_X(x) = P(X > x)\).

+--------------------------------------------+
|i3 : probability_Z(1.96, LowerTail => false)|
|                                            |
|o3 = .02499789514822037                     |
|                                            |
|o3 : RR (of precision 53)                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
* Menu:

* LowerTail::                   whether to computer lower tail probabilities

Ways to use probability:
========================

  * "probability(DiscreteProbabilityDistribution,Number)"
  * "probability(ProbabilityDistribution,Number)"

