File: Probability.info, Node: quantile, Up: QuantileFunction

quantile -- quantile function
*****************************

  * Usage: 
        quantile_X p
  * Inputs:
      * X, a *note probability distribution: ProbabilityDistribution,, 
      * p, a *note real number: (Macaulay2Doc)RR,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * LowerTail => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true
  * Outputs:
      * a *note real number: (Macaulay2Doc)RR,, 

Description
===========

For continuous probability distributions, the quantile function (see
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/quantile_function ) is the inverse of the
cumulative distribution function, i.e., \(x\) for which \(P(X \leq x) = p\).

+--------------------------------------+
|i1 : Z = normalDistribution()         |
|                                      |
|o1 = N(0,1)                           |
|                                      |
|o1 : ContinuousProbabilityDistribution|
+--------------------------------------+
|i2 : quantile_Z 0.95                  |
|                                      |
|o2 = 1.644853626951472                |
|                                      |
|o2 : RR (of precision 53)             |
+--------------------------------------+
|i3 : probability_Z oo                 |
|                                      |
|o3 = .95                              |
|                                      |
|o3 : RR (of precision 53)             |
+--------------------------------------+

For discrete probability distributions, it returns the smallest \(x\) for which
\(P(X \leq x) \geq p\).

+---------------------------------------+
|i4 : X = binomialDistribution(10, 0.25)|
|                                       |
|o4 = B(10,.25)                         |
|                                       |
|o4 : DiscreteProbabilityDistribution   |
+---------------------------------------+
|i5 : quantile_X 0.75                   |
|                                       |
|o5 = 3                                 |
+---------------------------------------+
|i6 : probability_X 2                   |
|                                       |
|o6 = .5255928039550778                 |
|                                       |
|o6 : RR (of precision 53)              |
+---------------------------------------+
|i7 : probability_X 3                   |
|                                       |
|o7 = .7758750915527344                 |
|                                       |
|o7 : RR (of precision 53)              |
+---------------------------------------+

If the LowerTail option is false, then it instead finds \(x\) for which \(P(X >
x) = p\) in the continuous case.

+------------------------------------------+
|i8 : quantile_Z(0.95, LowerTail => false) |
|                                          |
|o8 = -1.644853626951472                   |
|                                          |
|o8 : RR (of precision 53)                 |
+------------------------------------------+
|i9 : probability_Z(oo, LowerTail => false)|
|                                          |
|o9 = .95                                  |
|                                          |
|o9 : RR (of precision 53)                 |
+------------------------------------------+

In the discrete case, it finds the smallest \(x\) for which \(P(X > x) \leq
p\).

+------------------------------------------+
|i10 : quantile_X(0.75, LowerTail => false)|
|                                          |
|o10 = 2                                   |
+------------------------------------------+
|i11 : probability_X(2, LowerTail => false)|
|                                          |
|o11 = .4744071960449222                   |
|                                          |
|o11 : RR (of precision 53)                |
+------------------------------------------+
|i12 : probability_X(1, LowerTail => false)|
|                                          |
|o12 = .7559747695922852                   |
|                                          |
|o12 : RR (of precision 53)                |
+------------------------------------------+

